Four process variables were selected for systematic investigation: sulfuric acid concentration (X 1 : 1–5 M), hydrazine hydrate dosage (X 2 : 2–14 wt.% relative to solid mass), leaching temperature (X 3 : 25–85 °C), and leaching duration (X 4 : 2–6 h). Their individual and combined effects on the extraction efficiencies of Zn, Cu, Cd, and Fe were evaluated using RSM structured around a CCD. The CCD consisted of 2 4  = 16 full-factorial points, eight axial points positioned at a rotatability constant of  α  = 2.0, and five replicated center points—29 runs in total. This configuration provides uniform prediction variance across the design space and supports the estimation of curvature, which a standard factorial design cannot accommodate. A second-order polynomial of the form 1 Y = β 0 + Σ β i X i + Σ β ij X i 2 + Σ β ij X i X j + ε \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
